import{t as e}from"./utils-COoNfFoj.js";var t={slug:`alec-steele`,influencerName:`Alec Steele`,influencerHandle:`@alecsteele`,influencerAvatarUrl:`${e}/alec-steele-avatar.jpg`,multiAsset:!0,heroTitle:`Alec's Computer Builds`,assets:[{title:`Forging Blow Visualiser`,imageUrl:`${e}/alec-steele-forging-blow-visualiser.png`,quote:`Software exists to calculate reduction rates of forgings, but it’s relegated to the computers of engineers in big factories. I’ve always wanted to play with a forging app that simulates the forging characteristics of steel, so I built my own to compare the forging of different size, stroke and speed of power hammers on different bar dimensions and temperatures. Not only is it interesting from a technical point of view to visualise these comparisons, it is also delightfully satisfying to see the hot steel be squished on the screen.`,promptText:`Make me an interactive forging simulator where I can change the power hammer ram weight, beats per minute, stroke length, stock size, and stock temperature, then see how each blow deforms the material. Use a simple square-section bar as the default, maybe 50 mm × 50 mm and 100 mm long, but let me change the dimensions. I want an animated ram striking the workpiece, with buttons for a single hit or a 10-hit burst at the selected hammer speed, and I want the tool to show the deformation after each blow. Research how BPM relates to real ram impact speed, because the downstroke speed on a reciprocating hammer is probably not just the stroke distance divided by the beats per minute. Include adjustable stroke length, metric/imperial toggles, Celsius/Fahrenheit toggles, and clear outputs showing deformation per blow and total deformation after multiple blows.`,remixUrl:`https://www.perplexity.ai/computer/a/forging-blow-visualiser-PwL4ZUErQe.qfVLeol.ctw`},{title:`ForgeTreat`,imageUrl:`${e}/alec-steele-forgetreat.png`,quote:`It took me years to get to grips with all the different steel grades out there and I thought it was about time an intuitive tool existed to convert between them and display their heat-treatment information clearly. This should save a lot of time picking material for the next job and hopefully even save some money with the recurring price-checking function.`,promptText:`Make me an interactive heat-treating tool that helps me understand heat treatment and find useful heat-treat parameters for common steel grades in the UK, EU, and USA. I want to search steel grades, see equivalent or near-equivalent grades across different standards, compare two steels side by side, and visualise their chemical composition. You should research and find the most trustworthy sources to base your models on and double-check all assumptions. The tool should explain hardening, tempering, annealing, normalising, soak times, quench options, tempering temperatures, and temper cycles in a way that is easy to understand. Let me input the grade, cross-section size, austenitising temperature, soak time, quench medium, tempering temperature, temper duration, and number of temper cycles, then show the likely resulting characteristics such as hardness, toughness, brittleness risk, distortion risk, and suitable applications.`,remixUrl:`https://www.perplexity.ai/computer/a/forgetreat-fP5t78APT8Ck6CKY2lZmuA`},{title:`Workshop Reorder Assistant`,imageUrl:`${e}/alec-steele-workshop-reorder-assistant.png`,quote:`Running my workshop requires a lot of online ordering. Strange tooling is arriving from all over the web to make my projects happen. I often lose track of where I got it or what it’s called when it’s time to reorder. I wanted an app to scan all my purchases from my Gmail, import all the supplies I’ve ever purchased for the shop into a database, and let me easily search for and buy what I need at the snap of a photo and click of a button. I am always in a rush, and every minute spent searching through tool catalogues for part numbers is time I am not being productive.`,promptText:`Connect to my work Gmail and research all the tools, inserts, consumables, and workshop supplies I have purchased from receipts, order confirmations, invoices, and supplier emails. Build a searchable database with product names, SKUs, part numbers, suppliers, purchase links, quantities, dates, prices, order numbers, and categories. Then make a reorder assistant that helps me quickly find things I have already bought. I want to be able to say something like, “I snapped this lathe insert,” take a photo from my phone without saving it to my camera roll, and have the tool analyse the image, compare it with my purchase history, and suggest the most likely replacement, plus the second, third, and fourth most likely matches, with reorder links, part numbers, confidence levels, and a short explanation of why each item was suggested.`,remixUrl:`https://www.perplexity.ai/computer/a/workshop-reorder-assistant-dem-cmUY6nkYTR2WRfEietYKtQ`}]};export{t as default};
